  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "In this essay, I reflect on four formative books that have shaped my work as a geographer. I discuss each of the books in the context where I first encountered them: Feminism & Geography as an undergraduate in Dublin, Ireland; The Wretched of the Earth as a graduate student in Kentucky, US; Even in Sweden as a new faculty member in Nottingham, UK; and Imperial Intimacies in my current role in Maynooth, Ireland. I write about my memories of how the books made me feel and think, and how they helped me find my place in the academy. In their unique ways, the books grapple with important issues such as feminism, colonialism and racism. Now, though, I also see that they share a concern with epistemology and contain the potential for hope. These four books offer political and passionate insights into how to write new worlds.",
